WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
What a test set is for.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:04.568
Lesson 2 gave you a ranked list of failure modes.

3
00:00:04.848 --> 00:00:16.155
A test set turns that list into something you can run: a versioned collection of inputs, each with what a good output must (and must not) do, that you score every time the system changes.

4
00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:19.144
Two kinds are worth keeping separate from the start.

5
00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:26.997
A golden set is a curated, representative sample of the work, with expert-approved expected outputs or criteria.

6
00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:31.736
It changes rarely and deliberately, and it is what you report as “quality.

7
00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:37.029
” A regression set is a growing collection of cases that once failed and were fixed.

8
00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:43.261
It should pass at or near 100 percent, and a failure there means something you fixed has broken again.

9
00:00:43.541 --> 00:00:59.733
This mirrors Anthropic’s distinction between capability evals (room to climb) and regression evals (should stay near 100 percent), and the evals FAQ makes the same point that evals, like unit tests, catch problems that come back after a fix.

10
00:01:00.013 --> 00:01:01.357
Start small and real.

11
00:01:01.637 --> 00:01:14.117
Anthropic’s evals post says 20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures is a good start, and that a good task is one where two domain experts would independently reach the same verdict.

12
00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:21.693
Your first golden set can be the 36 traces from this week’s lab, with the expert’s labels as the expected outcomes.

13
00:01:21.973 --> 00:01:25.984
There are three places cases come from, and a good set uses all three.
